---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 11: Smart Servo Control - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 11: Smart Servo Control</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Smooth non-blocking motion and serial commands on two SG90 servos</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🦾 Everything you build today is exactly what the robot arm will run on — no printed parts needed yet</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Lesson Goals:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Replace <code>delay()</code> with non-blocking <code>millis()</code> timing</li>
                        <li>• Ramp two servos smoothly to target angles at the same time</li>
                        <li>• Build a Serial Monitor command interface (<code>B90</code>, <code>E45</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>POSE n</code>)</li>
                        <li>• Store preset poses using the <code>struct</code> arrays from Lesson 9.5</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Skills Developed:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Timing patterns used in every real robot program</li>
                        <li>• Serial input parsing and simple text commands</li>
                        <li>• Coordinated motion and safe angle limits</li>
                        <li>• Software that transfers straight onto the robot arm later</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Parts Inventory -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 1: Parts Inventory</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Electronic Components:</h4>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Arduino Uno microcontroller</li>
                        <li>• 2x SG90 micro servos (call them "base" and "elbow" — they stay loose on your desk)</li>
                        <li>• Breadboard (or expansion board) + jumper wires</li>
                        <li>• Push button (A2 to GND, INPUT_PULLUP)</li>
                        <li>• Potentiometer (A0) for angle control</li>
                        <li>• Buzzer (optional) for command feedback</li>
                        <li>• USB cable (also used for the Serial Monitor commands)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fff7ed; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Mechanical Parts:</h4>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• <strong>None required this week!</strong> The servos can sit loose on the table</li>
                        <li>• (Optional) Tape or poster tack to keep servo bodies from wandering</li>
                        <li>• (Optional) Paperclip or pointer taped to a servo horn to make motion easier to see</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
                        The 3D printed arm parts are still being designed. When they arrive, this exact program becomes the arm's control software — that's why we label the servos "base" and "elbow" now.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Pin Map -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📌 Section 2: Standard Pin Map</h2>
            
            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Same pin map as always — this is the map the robot arm will use too</h4>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                    <li><strong>Button:</strong> A2 (to GND, INPUT_PULLUP)</li>
                    <li><strong>Potentiometer:</strong> A0</li>
                    <li><strong>Buzzer (optional):</strong> pin 8</li>
                    <li><strong>Base servo signal:</strong> pin 9</li>
                    <li><strong>Elbow servo signal:</strong> pin 10</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- delay vs millis -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">⏱️ Section 3: Why <code>delay()</code> Breaks Robot Programs</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Up to now, every servo move you wrote probably used <code>delay()</code> to pause between steps. That works for a single servo — but <code>delay()</code> literally <strong>freezes the entire chip</strong>. While it waits, the button can't be read, the second servo can't move, and serial commands are ignored. A robot arm that must move two joints smoothly can't afford to freeze.
            </p>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                The fix is the <strong>non-blocking timing pattern</strong>: instead of sleeping, we check a clock. <code>millis()</code> returns how many milliseconds have passed since the program started. We remember the last time we did work, and only do the next step when enough time has elapsed:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`unsigned long lastStepTime = 0;
const int stepInterval = 15;   // ms between motion steps

void loop() {
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        // do ONE small step of work here
    }
    // the rest of loop() keeps running — buttons,
    // serial, second servo all stay responsive
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef3c7; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4 style="color: #92400e; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">💡 The Microwave Analogy</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    <code>delay(2000)</code> is like standing in front of the microwave watching it count down — you can't do anything else. <code>millis()</code> is like glancing at the clock while you do other things, and checking the microwave when it's time. Same 2 seconds, but your Arduino stays useful the whole time.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Smooth motion -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🤏 Section 4: Smooth, Coordinated Motion</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                A jerky robot jumps straight to its target angle. A smooth robot <strong>steps toward the target one degree at a time</strong>. We keep two numbers per servo: its current angle and its target angle. On every timed step, each servo moves one degree closer — and because both servos step in the same tick, they move together even when their distances differ.
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`int baseAngle   = 90;   // where the servo is now
int elbowAngle  = 90;
int baseTarget  = 90;   // where we want it to go
int elbowTarget = 90;

int moveToward(int current, int target) {
    if (current < target) return current + 1;
    if (current > target) return current - 1;
    return current;   // already there
}

void loop() {
    if (millis() - lastStepTime >= stepInterval) {
        lastStepTime = millis();
        baseAngle  = moveToward(baseAngle,  baseTarget);
        elbowAngle = moveToward(elbowAngle, elbowTarget);
        baseServo.write(baseAngle);
        elbowServo.write(elbowAngle);
    }
    // ... inputs and serial commands handled below,
    // they only change the TARGETS
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1rem;">
                Notice the separation: <strong>inputs change targets, the timed step moves servos</strong>. That's the core architecture of nearly every robot program you'll ever write.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Serial commands -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📟 Section 5: Serial Command Interface</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                Now for the fun part: typing commands into Serial Monitor to drive the servos. Our mini language:
            </p>

            <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.9;">
                    <li><code>B120</code> → set base target to 120°</li>
                    <li><code>E45</code> → set elbow target to 45°</li>
                    <li><code>HOME</code> → both servos return to 90°</li>
                    <li><code>POSE 2</code> → run preset pose #2 (next section)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`void handleSerial() {
    if (Serial.available() == 0) return;   // nothing typed? do nothing

    String cmd = Serial.readStringUntil('\n');
    cmd.trim();        // remove spaces and the newline
    cmd.toUpperCase(); // "b90" and "B90" both work

    if (cmd.startsWith("B")) {
        baseTarget = constrain(cmd.substring(1).toInt(), 20, 160);
        Serial.println("Base target: " + String(baseTarget));
    } else if (cmd.startsWith("E")) {
        elbowTarget = constrain(cmd.substring(1).toInt(), 20, 160);
        Serial.println("Elbow target: " + String(elbowTarget));
    } else if (cmd == "HOME") {
        baseTarget = 90;
        elbowTarget = 90;
        Serial.println("Going home");
    } else {
        Serial.println("Unknown command: " + cmd);
    }
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Serial Monitor Settings</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Set the Serial Monitor to <strong>9600 baud</strong> (matching <code>Serial.begin(9600)</code>) and line ending to <strong>"Newline"</strong> or <strong>"Both NL &amp; CR"</strong> — <code>readStringUntil('\n')</code> needs that newline to know your command is finished.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Preset poses -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Section 6: Preset Poses with Structs</h2>
            
            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                In <a href="/programming/lessons32/lesson9-5" style="color: #667eea; font-weight: 600;">Lesson 9.5</a> you learned that a <code>struct</code> groups related values, and an array of structs stores a whole table of them. A robot pose is exactly that — a name plus an angle for each joint:
            </p>

            <div style="position: relative;">
                <button onclick="copyCode(this)" style="position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; background-color: #2563eb; color: white; border: none; padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; font-size: 12px; z-index: 10;">📋 Copy</button>
                <pre style="background: #1e293b; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 10px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.6;"><code>{`struct Pose {
    const char* name;
    int base;
    int elbow;
};

Pose poses[] = {
    {"HOME",  90, 90},
    {"READY", 90, 45},
    {"REACH", 45, 20}
};
const int numPoses = sizeof(poses) / sizeof(poses[0]);

// inside handleSerial():
} else if (cmd.startsWith("POSE")) {
    int i = cmd.substring(5).toInt();      // "POSE 2" -> 2
    if (i >= 0 && i < numPoses) {
        baseTarget  = poses[i].base;
        elbowTarget = poses[i].elbow;
        Serial.println("Pose: " + String(poses[i].name));
    }
}`}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.7; margin-top: 1rem;">
                <strong>Extension challenge:</strong> make the A2 button step through the pose list — each press advances to the next pose and plays a buzzer chirp. (Hint: <code>poseIndex = (poseIndex + 1) % numPoses;</code>)
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Wiring and Connections -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🔌 Wiring and Connections</h2>
            
            <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; border-left: 4px solid #dc2626;">
                <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">⚠️ Safety First!</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
                    Always disconnect power before making any connections. SG90 servos can draw bursts of current. If a servo jitters or resets your Arduino,
                    consider powering the servos from a separate 5V source (and connect grounds).
                </p>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Power Connections</h3>
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Start with a single servo powered from Arduino 5V (test)</li>
                        <li>• If the Arduino resets or the servo jitters, use an external 5V supply for servos</li>
                        <li>• Always connect all grounds together (Arduino GND ↔ servo power supply GND)</li>
                        <li>• Verify voltage levels before powering on</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Signal Connections</h3>
                <div style="background: #f0f9ff; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Base servo signal → pin 9</li>
                        <li>• Elbow servo signal → pin 10</li>
                        <li>• Button → A2 to GND (INPUT_PULLUP)</li>
                        <li>• Potentiometer wiper → A0 (other pins to 5V and GND)</li>
                        <li>• (Optional) Buzzer → pin 8 to GND</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Testing and Troubleshooting -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🧪 Testing and Troubleshooting</h2>
            
            <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Initial Testing Checklist</h3>
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>✅ Visual inspection of all servo wires (signal/power/ground)</li>
                        <li>✅ Test each servo individually with the Lesson 10 “basic servo control” sketch</li>
                        <li>✅ Serial Monitor set to 9600 baud with Newline line-ending</li>
                        <li>✅ Try commands in order: <code>B60</code>, <code>E120</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>POSE 1</code></li>
                        <li>✅ Confirm the button still responds while a servo is mid-move (that proves your timing is non-blocking)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Common Issues and Solutions</h3>
                <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem;">
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Servo jitters</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Use a separate 5V supply for servos (shared ground), and avoid long loose jumper wires</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Arduino resets when servo moves</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">The servo is drawing too much current from Arduino 5V—use external 5V for servos</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Servo moves in the wrong direction</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Reverse the angle mapping in code or re-mount the servo horn after centering at 90°</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Servo doesn’t move at all</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Check the signal pin number, ensure servo ground is connected, and verify power polarity</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Serial commands are ignored</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">Check the baud rate (9600), set the line ending to Newline, and confirm the command starts with B, E, HOME, or POSE</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="background: #fef2f2; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                        <h4 style="color: #dc2626; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Problem: Button stops working while servo moves</h4>
                        <p style="color: #4a5568; margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;">A <code>delay()</code> sneaked back into the loop — every wait must use the millis() pattern instead</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Assessment & Next Steps -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📝 Assessment & Next Steps</h2>
            
            <div style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Lesson 11 Verification</h3>
                <div style="background: #f8fafc; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                        Before moving on, make sure your smart servo controller works:
                    </p>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>✅ Both servos ramp smoothly to targets (no jumps, no delay() anywhere)</li>
                        <li>📟 Serial commands B, E, HOME, and POSE all work from Serial Monitor</li>
                        <li>🔘 Button stays responsive while servos are mid-move</li>
                        <li>⚡ Power is stable (no resets during movement)</li>
                        <li>🛡️ Angles are clamped to safe limits (20°–160°)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
                <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">🎯 Where This Leads</h4>
                <p style="color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">
                    Next week we switch gears to the <strong>miniAuto car</strong> — but keep this sketch. The <code>millis()</code> timing pattern shows up everywhere in robotics, and when the 3D printed arm parts are ready, this program becomes the arm's control software almost unchanged: the poses you typed today become the poses the arm strikes.
                </p>
            </div>

